  "task": {
    "name": "Escalation-history decay \u2014 age out stale escalations from _check_promotion matching",
    "slug": "issue-1879",
    "story_path": null,
    "story_text": "## Observed / motivation\n\n`assignment.py:_check_promotion` bumps a story's dev tier upward when 2+ of the last 10 matching escalation records are `ESCALATE`. The matching slice is recency-bounded (last 10 records) but never *decays* by age: a single outlier escalation persists in that window for a long time on infrequent complexity bands, holding the tier promoted even after subsequent runs succeed cleanly.\n\nThis is one of the open asymmetries catalogued by the routing-symmetry invariant (#1389). The promotion path exists; the compensating \"let stale escalations stop holding the tier up\" path does not.\n\n## Why\n\nPromotion stickiness with no decay is a structural driver of the strong-tier over-allocation documented in #1387's audit data. Age-decaying the escalation window is the demotion-adjacent mechanism that lets a clean recent track record overcome an old one-off escalation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- `_check_promotion` excludes escalation records older than a defined threshold (record count and/or age in days) from its matching slice, so stale escalations no longer count toward the promotion trigger.\n- The decay threshold is a named, documented condition (not a magic literal buried in code).\n- The routing decision audit records when decay changed the matching set (how many records were excluded by age), consistent with the routing-symmetry audit-attribution requirement.\n- Tests cover both directions: an old escalation that is decayed out (no promotion) and a recent escalation cluster that still promotes.\n- The `ROUTING_SYMMETRY_REGISTRY` entry and `docs/routing-symmetry-followups.md` are updated to reflect this asymmetry as resolved.\n\n## Example\n\nA medium-band story escalated once 40 runs ago, then completed cleanly on the last 8 runs. Today `_check_promotion` can still see that lone escalation inside the last-10 slice on an infrequent band and keep the tier promoted. With decay, the 40-runs-old escalation is excluded and the clean recent record keeps the story at its score-band tier.\n\nFollow-up to #1389 (routing-symmetry invariant). Companion to #1387 (post-plan checkpoint demotion).",
    "github_issue": 1879,
    "fix_ready": true,
    "readiness_warnings": []
  },
